.TP
\fBUNICODE(text)\fR \(-> number
the code point of the first character
.TP
\fBCLEAN(text)\fR \(-> text
remove non-printing characters
.TP
\fBCONCAT(text1, \[u2026])\fR \(-> text
join values as text (same as &)
.TP
\fBCONCATENATE(text1, \[u2026])\fR \(-> text
join values as text
.TP
\fBEXACT(text1, text2)\fR \(-> boolean
case-sensitive equality
.TP
\fBFIND(find_text, within_text, [start_num=1])\fR \(-> number
1-based position of find_text in within_text, case-sensitive
.TP
\fBSEARCH(find_text, within_text, [start_num=1])\fR \(-> number
1-based position, case-insensitive, with * and ? wildcards
.TP
\fBLEFT(text, [num_chars=1])\fR \(-> text
the first num_chars characters (default 1)
.TP
\fBRIGHT(text, [num_chars=1])\fR \(-> text
the last num_chars characters (default 1)
.TP
\fBMID(text, start_num, num_chars)\fR \(-> text
num_chars characters from a 1-based start_num
.TP
\fBLEN(text)\fR \(-> number
the number of characters
.TP
\fBLOWER(text)\fR \(-> text
lowercase
.TP
\fBUPPER(text)\fR \(-> text
uppercase
.TP
\fBPROPER(text)\fR \(-> text
capitalize each word
.TP
\fBREPLACE(old_text, start_num, num_chars, new_text)\fR \(-> text
replace num_chars characters from a 1-based start_num
.TP
\fBREPT(text, number_times)\fR \(-> text
repeat text number_times times
.TP
\fBSUBSTITUTE(text, old_text, new_text, [instance_num])\fR \(-> text
replace every (or the instance_num-th) occurrence of old_text
.TP
\fBTRIM(text)\fR \(-> text
remove leading, trailing and repeated spaces
.TP
\fBVALUE(text)\fR \(-> number
text as a number
.TP
\fBTEXTBEFORE(text, delimiter, [instance_num=1], [match_mode=0], [match_end=0], [if_not_found])\fR \(-> text
text before the instance_num-th delimiter
.TP
\fBTEXTAFTER(text, delimiter, [instance_num=1], [match_mode=0], [match_end=0], [if_not_found])\fR \(-> text
text after the instance_num-th delimiter
.TP
\fBTEXTBETWEEN(text, start_delimiter, end_delimiter)\fR \(-> text
text between the first start delimiter and the next end delimiter
.TP
\fBCOUNTMATCHES(text, search)\fR \(-> number
how many times search occurs in text
.TP
\fBTEXT(value, format_text)\fR \(-> text
format with an Excel number-format code: 0 # ? . , % E+ E- "text" \ec _c *c, General, ; sections (positive;negative;zero;text with @), colors; dates y m d h s AM/PM A/P. Unsupported (#VALUE!): conditions, locales, [h] elapsed time, fractional seconds, fractions, eras
.TP
\fBFIXED(number, [decimals=2], [no_commas=FALSE])\fR \(-> text
rounded to decimals places (default 2) as text, with thousands commas unless no_commas
.TP
\fBDOLLAR(number, [decimals=2])\fR \(-> text
as en-US currency text: $1,234.57, negatives in parentheses, decimals default 2
.TP
\fBNUMBERVALUE(text, [decimal_separator="."], [group_separator=","])\fR \(-> number
text as a number with explicit decimal (default .) and group (default ,) separators
.TP
\fBVALUETOTEXT(value, [format=0])\fR \(-> text
a value as text; format 1 quotes text
.TP
\fBREGEX(text, expression, [replacement], [flags])\fR \(-> text
the first match (#N/A if none), or with a replacement the text with the first match replaced; the fourth argument is "g" (replace all) or the occurrence to replace. Patterns use the Rust regex syntax, not ICU's
.TP
\fBREGEXTEST(text, pattern, [case_sensitivity=0])\fR \(-> boolean
whether the pattern matches; case_sensitivity 1 ignores case. Rust regex syntax
.TP
\fBREGEXEXTRACT(text, pattern, [return_mode=0], [case_sensitivity=0])\fR \(-> text
the first match (#N/A if none); only return mode 0, modes 1 and 2 (arrays) are #VALUE!. Rust regex syntax (also \fBREGEX.EXTRACT\fR)
.TP
\fBREGEXREPLACE(text, pattern, replacement, [occurrence=0], [case_sensitivity=0])\fR \(-> text
replace every match (or the occurrence-th, negative from the end) with $n-style replacement text. Rust regex syntax
.TP
\fBTEXTSPLIT(text OVER T, col_delimiter, [row_delimiter], [ignore_empty=FALSE] INTO K)\fR \(-> text
the parts of the text between col_delimiter matches (case-sensitive), in order; written without OVER, each cell splits on its own: TEXTSPLIT(Text, "," INTO Part). row_delimiter must be "" (splitting into rows is not supported yet); ignore_empty drops empty parts; empty text is #VALUE!
.TP
\fBARRAYTOTEXT(array OVER T, [format=0])\fR \(-> text
the values as text in item order: format 0 "1, 2, a", format 1 {1,2,"a"}
.TP
\fBASC(text)\fR \(-> text
full-width ASCII and katakana as half-width, as LibreOffice's ASC (a voiced kana becomes its letter and a sound mark)
.TP
\fBJIS(text)\fR \(-> text
half-width ASCII and katakana as full-width, as LibreOffice's JIS (a letter and its sound mark join) (also \fBDBCS\fR)
.TP
\fBBAHTTEXT(number)\fR \(-> text
a number as Thai baht and satang in words, rounded to the satang
.TP
\fBROT13(text)\fR \(-> text
ASCII letters rotated 13 places; anything else unchanged
.TP
\fBENCODEURL(text)\fR \(-> text
UTF-8 percent-encoding of every byte but A\[u2013]Z a\[u2013]z 0\[u2013]9 - _ . ~ (#VALUE! for empty text)
.TP
\fBROMAN(number, [form=0])\fR \(-> text
0\[u2013]3999 in Roman numerals; form 0 classic (or TRUE) to 4 most concise (or FALSE)
.TP
\fBARABIC(text)\fR \(-> number
Roman numerals (0\[u2013]3999) as a number; case and surrounding spaces ignored, a leading - negates, "" is 0
.SH "DATE AND TIME"
Dates are date-time values (UTC, millisecond precision). A number given where a date is wanted is a spreadsheet serial date (1 = 1900-01-01).
.TP
\fBDATE(year, month, day)\fR \(-> date
a date; out-of-range months and days roll over, years 0\[u2013]1899 add 1900
.TP
\fBDATEVALUE(date_text)\fR \(-> date
parse a date: 2025-01-31, 2025-01-31T09:30:00Z (the time is dropped) or 1/31/2025
.TP
\fBTIME(hour, minute, second)\fR \(-> date
a time of day on serial day 0 (1899-12-30), wrapping at 24 hours
.TP
\fBTIMEVALUE(time_text)\fR \(-> number
parse a time (09:30, 9:30 PM, 2025-01-31T09:30:00Z) as a fraction of a day
.TP
\fBYEAR(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the year
.TP
\fBMONTH(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the month, 1\[u2013]12
.TP
\fBDAY(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the day of the month
.TP
\fBHOUR(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the hour, 0\[u2013]23
.TP
\fBMINUTE(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the minute
.TP
\fBSECOND(serial_number)\fR \(-> number
the second, rounded
.TP
\fBWEEKDAY(serial_number, [return_type=1])\fR \(-> number
day of the week: type 1 Sunday=1 (default), 2 Monday=1, 3 Monday=0, 11\[u2013]17 day 1 is Monday\[u2026]Sunday
.TP
\fBWEEKNUM(serial_number, [return_type=1])\fR \(-> number
week of the year (the week of January 1 is 1), weeks starting on type's day as in WEEKDAY (1, 2, 11\[u2013]17); type 21 is the ISO week (also \fBWEEKNUM_EXCEL2003\fR)
.TP
\fBISOWEEKNUM(date)\fR \(-> number
the ISO 8601 week number
.TP
\fBISOYEAR(date)\fR \(-> number
the ISO 8601 week-numbering year
.TP
\fBDAYOFYEAR(date)\fR \(-> number
the day of the year, 1\[u2013]366
.TP
\fBDAYNAME(date)\fR \(-> text
the English weekday name of a date, or of a day number 1\[u2013]7 (1 is Sunday)
.TP
\fBMONTHNAME(month)\fR \(-> text
the English name of month 1\[u2013]12
.TP
\fBDAYS(end_date, start_date)\fR \(-> number
days from start to end (end \[u2212] start, times included)
.TP
\fBDAYS360(start_date, end_date, [method=FALSE])\fR \(-> number
days between on a 360-day year, US (NASD) method or European when TRUE
.TP
\fBDATEDIF(start_date, end_date, unit)\fR \(-> number
complete "Y" years, "M" months or "D" days between; "YM", "YD", "MD" ignore the larger units
.TP
\fBEDATE(start_date, months)\fR \(-> date
the same day n months later (clamped to the month's end)
.TP
\fBEOMONTH(start_date, months)\fR \(-> date
the last day of the month n months later
.TP
\fBYEARFRAC(start_date, end_date, [basis=0])\fR \(-> number
the fraction of a year between: basis 0 US 30/360 (default), 1 actual/actual, 2 actual/360, 3 actual/365, 4 European 30/360
.TP
\fBNETWORKDAYS(start_date, end_date)\fR \(-> number
working days (Monday\[u2013]Friday) from start to end inclusive; no holidays argument yet (it needs an aggregation) (also \fBNETWORKDAYS_EXCEL2003\fR)
.TP
\fBNETWORKDAYS.INTL(start_date, end_date, [weekend=1])\fR \(-> number
working days with a weekend of 1\[u2013]7 (two days), 11\[u2013]17 (one day) or a "0000011" Monday-first mask; no holidays argument yet
.TP
\fBWORKDAY(start_date, days)\fR \(-> date
the date n working days (Monday\[u2013]Friday) after start; no holidays argument yet
.TP
\fBWORKDAY.INTL(start_date, days, [weekend=1])\fR \(-> date
WORKDAY with a weekend as in NETWORKDAYS.INTL; no holidays argument yet
.TP
\fBNOW()\fR \(-> date
the current date and time (UTC); re-evaluated when the model is recalculated
.TP
\fBTODAY()\fR \(-> date
the current date (UTC midnight); re-evaluated when the model is recalculated
.TP
\fBDAYSINMONTH(date)\fR \(-> number
the number of days in the date's month
.TP
\fBDAYSINYEAR(date)\fR \(-> number
the number of days in the date's year
.TP
\fBWEEKSINYEAR(date)\fR \(-> number
the number of ISO weeks (52 or 53) in the date's year
.TP
\fBISLEAPYEAR(date)\fR \(-> boolean
whether the date's year is a leap year
.TP
\fBMONTHS(start_date, end_date, type)\fR \(-> number
months between: type 0 complete months, 1 calendar months
.TP
\fBWEEKS(start_date, end_date, type)\fR \(-> number
weeks between: type 0 complete weeks, 1 calendar (Monday) weeks
.TP
\fBYEARS(start_date, end_date, type)\fR \(-> number
years between: type 0 complete years, 1 calendar years
.TP
\fBUNIX2DATE(t)\fR \(-> date
the date of a Unix time in seconds
.TP
\fBDATE2UNIX(d)\fR \(-> number
a date as Unix time in seconds
.TP
\fBDUR2DAYS(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration (a number of days) in days
.TP
\fBDUR2HOURS(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration in days as hours
.TP
\fBDUR2MINUTES(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration in days as minutes
.TP
\fBDUR2SECONDS(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration in days as seconds
.TP
\fBDUR2MILLISECONDS(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration in days as milliseconds
.TP
\fBDUR2WEEKS(duration)\fR \(-> number
a duration in days as weeks
.TP
\fBSTRIPDURATION(duration)\fR \(-> number
the value (durations are already a number of days)
.TP
\fBEASTERSUNDAY(year)\fR \(-> date
Easter Sunday (Gregorian) of a year 1583\[u2013]9956; 0\[u2013]29 mean 2000\[u2013]2029, 30\[u2013]99 mean 1930\[u2013]1999
.TP
\fBASCENSIONTHURSDAY(year)\fR \(-> date
Ascension Thursday, 39 days after Easter Sunday
.TP
\fBASHWEDNESDAY(year)\fR \(-> date
Ash Wednesday, 46 days before Easter Sunday
.TP
\fBGOODFRIDAY(year)\fR \(-> date
Good Friday, 2 days before Easter Sunday
.TP
\fBPENTECOSTSUNDAY(year)\fR \(-> date
Pentecost Sunday, 49 days after Easter Sunday
.TP
\fBDATE2JULIAN(date)\fR \(-> number
the Julian day number of a date
.TP
\fBHDATE_JULIAN(year, month, day)\fR \(-> number
the Julian day number of Gregorian year, month, day (out-of-range days and months roll over)
.TP
\fBHDATE(year, month, day)\fR \(-> text
the Hebrew date of Gregorian year, month, day, as "20 Tebet 5784" (#VALUE! for a month outside 1\[u2013]12 or a day outside 1\[u2013]31)
.TP
\fBHDATE_HEB(year, month, day)\fR \(-> text
the Hebrew date of Gregorian year, month, day in Hebrew, as "\[u05DB]\[u05F3] \[u05D1]\[u05B0]\[u05BC]\[u05D8]\[u05B5]\[u05D1]\[u05B5]\[u05EA] \[u05D4]\[u05EA]\[u05E9]\[u05E4]\[u05F4]\[u05D3]"
.TP
\fBHDATE_DAY(year, month, day)\fR \(-> number
the day of the Hebrew month of Gregorian year, month, day
.TP
\fBHDATE_MONTH(year, month, day)\fR \(-> number
the Hebrew month of Gregorian year, month, day: 0 Tishri \[u2026] 11 Elul, 12 Adar I, 13 Adar II
.TP
\fBHDATE_YEAR(year, month, day)\fR \(-> number
the Hebrew year of Gregorian year, month, day
.TP
\fBDATE2HDATE(date)\fR \(-> text
the Hebrew date of a date, as "20 Tebet 5784"
.TP
\fBDATE2HDATE_HEB(date)\fR \(-> text
the Hebrew date of a date in Hebrew
.SH "DIMENSIONAL"
Functions of a cell's position in a category, and the ordered (previous/next, running) forms.
.TP
\fBPOSITION(category)\fR \(-> number
1-based position of this cell's item along the category
.TP
\fB@ITEM(category)\fR \(-> text
the name of this cell's item along the category
.TP
\fB@ID(category)\fR \(-> text
the item name (Improv item names are unique within a category)
.TP
\fB@ITEMCOUNT(category)\fR \(-> number
the number of items in the category
.TP
\fBPREV(x, category, [n=1], [default])\fR \(-> the branches' type
the value at the n-th previous item along the category (n = 1); off the end, the default, else no cell
.TP
\fBNEXT(x, category, [n=1], [default])\fR \(-> the branches' type
the value at the n-th next item along the category (n = 1); off the end, the default, else no cell
.TP
\fBSEQUENCE(T, [start=1], [step=1])\fR \(-> the branches' type
SEQUENCE(T, [start=1], [step=1]): start, start + step, \[u2026] along T's items, (POSITION(T) - 1) * step + start
.TP
\fBRELABEL(x, from_category, to_category)\fR \(-> the branches' type
x read with category From replaced by To, each item mapped to To's item of the same name (items with no namesake are dropped)
.TP
\fBSWAP(x, category_a, category_b)\fR \(-> the branches' type
x read with categories A and B exchanged, items matched by name (W at Src = a, Dst = b is SWAP(W, Src, Dst) at Src = b, Dst = a)
.TP
\fBFIXPOINT(init, lambda, [max_iter=100], [tolerance=1e-10])\fR \(-> the branches' type
FIXPOINT(init, LAMBDA(r, body), [max_iter=100], [tolerance=1e-10]): r = init, then r = body(r) until the cells change by at most tolerance in total (\[u03A3]|\[u0394]|), or max_iter times (then the last iterate); a measure's whole formula
.TP
\fBFILL(x, default)\fR \(-> the branches' type
a cell at every item combination of x's categories: x where it has a cell, else the default
.TP
\fBSORT(array OVER T, [sort_order=1] INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
the values in ascending order (descending when sort_order is -1)
.TP
\fBUNIQUE(array OVER T, [exactly_once=FALSE] INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
each distinct value once, in item order (only values that occur once when exactly_once is TRUE)
.TP
\fBFILTER(array, include OVER T, [if_empty] INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
the values of array whose include is TRUE, in item order; if none, if_empty (else #N/A: Excel's #CALC!)
.TP
\fBSORTBY(array, by_array1 OVER T, [sort_order1], \[u2026] INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
SORTBY(array, by_array1 OVER T, [sort_order1], [by_array2, sort_order2], \[u2026]): the values of array sorted by by_array1, ties by by_array2, \[u2026] (stable; a key descends when its order is -1)
.TP
\fBTAKE(array OVER T, rows INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
the first rows values in item order (the last -rows when rows is negative)
.TP
\fBDROP(array OVER T, rows INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
the values TAKE leaves out: all but the first rows (the last -rows when rows is negative), in item order
.TP
\fBSELECTS(values, tests OVER T, condition INTO K)\fR \(-> the branches' type
Quantrix: the values whose test meets the condition (a criterion like ">5" or "a*", as in SUMIF), in item order
.TP
\fBXLOOKUP(lookup_value, lookup_array OVER T, return_array, [if_not_found], [match_mode=0], [search_mode=1])\fR \(-> the values' type
return_array at the item where lookup_array matches lookup_value (match_mode 0 exact, -1 next smaller, 1 next larger, 2 wildcard; search_mode 1 first to last, -1 last to first, \[u00B1]2 the same as \[u00B1]1); if_not_found (else #N/A) when none does
.TP
\fBXMATCH(lookup_value, lookup_array OVER T, [match_mode=0], [search_mode=1])\fR \(-> number
the 1-based item position along the category where lookup_array matches lookup_value (XLOOKUP's modes); #N/A when none does
.TP
\fBMATCH(lookup_value, lookup_array OVER T, [match_type=1])\fR \(-> number
the 1-based item position of lookup_value: match_type 1 (default) the largest \[u2264] it, 0 the first equal (text with * and ? wildcards), -1 the smallest \[u2265] it; #N/A when none
.TP
\fBLOOKUP(lookup_value, lookup_vector OVER T, result_vector)\fR \(-> the values' type
result_vector at the largest lookup_vector \[u2264] lookup_value (ascending); #N/A when none
.TP
\fBINDEX(array OVER T, row_num)\fR \(-> the branches' type
the value at the row_num-th item (1-based); #REF! out of range, #N/A where that item has no cell (also \fBVALUEAT\fR)
.TP
\fBSELECT(values, keys OVER T, lookup)\fR \(-> the branches' type
values at the first item (in item order) whose key equals lookup; #N/A when none (SELECTLIST: a lookup over its own category gives one result per lookup) (also \fBSELECTLIST\fR)
.TP
\fBSELECTBETWEEN(values, keys OVER T, low, high, [inclusive])\fR \(-> the branches' type
values at the first item whose key is between low and high (strictly when inclusive is FALSE); #N/A when none
.TP
\fBSELECTGREATERTHAN(values, keys OVER T, lookup, [inclusive])\fR \(-> the branches' type
values at the first item whose key is > lookup (\[u2265] when inclusive is TRUE); #N/A when none
.TP
\fBSELECTLESSTHAN(values, keys OVER T, lookup, [inclusive])\fR \(-> the branches' type
values at the first item whose key is < lookup (\[u2264] when inclusive is TRUE); #N/A when none
.SH "STATISTICAL"
.TP
\fBMODE.MULT(values OVER T INTO K)\fR \(-> number
every most frequent value, in order of first occurrence (#N/A if none repeats)
.TP
\fBLINEST(known_ys, known_xs1, \[u2026] OVER T, [const=TRUE], [stats=FALSE] INTO K)\fR \(-> number
LINEST(known_ys, known_xs1, [known_xs2, \[u2026]] OVER T, [const], [stats]): the least-squares fit y = m1\[u00B7]x1 + \[u2026] + b: mk, \[u2026], m1, b (const = FALSE: b = 0); with stats, Excel's 5\[u00D7](k+1) block row by row (one x: m, b, se_m, se_b, r\[u00B2], se_y, F, df, ss_reg, ss_resid); INTO R, C lays it out as the block
.TP
\fBLOGEST(known_ys, known_xs1, \[u2026] OVER T, [const=TRUE], [stats=FALSE] INTO K)\fR \(-> number
LOGEST(known_ys, known_xs1, [known_xs2, \[u2026]] OVER T, [const], [stats]): y = b\[u00B7]m1^x1\[u00B7]\[u2026], fitted to ln y: mk, \[u2026], m1, b (const = FALSE: b = 1); with stats LINEST's block, for ln y
.TP
\fBFREQUENCY(data_array OVER T, bin1, \[u2026] INTO K)\fR \(-> number
counts of x in the bins given as numbers b1, b2, \[u2026]: \[u2264] b1, (b1, b2], \[u2026], > bn (n + 1 counts; Excel takes the bins as a range)
.TP
\fBBETA.DIST(x, alpha, beta, cumulative, [a=0], [b=1])\fR \(-> number
the beta distribution of x on [A, B] (default [0, 1]): density, or cumulative
.TP
\fBBETADIST(x, alpha, beta, [a=0], [b=1])\fR \(-> number
the cumulative beta distribution of x on [A, B] (default [0, 1])
.TP
\fBBETA.INV(probability, alpha, beta, [a=0], [b=1])\fR \(-> number
the beta quantile on [A, B] (default [0, 1]) (also \fBBETAINV\fR)
.TP
\fBBINOM.DIST(number_s, trials, probability_s, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the binomial probability of k successes in n trials: P(K = k), or P(K \[u2264] k) (also \fBBINOMDIST\fR)
.TP
\fBBINOM.INV(trials, probability_s, alpha)\fR \(-> number
the smallest k with binomial P(K \[u2264] k) \[u2265] alpha, for n trials of probability p (also \fBCRITBINOM\fR)
.TP
\fBBINOM.DIST.RANGE(trials, probability_s, number_s, [number_s2])\fR \(-> number
the binomial P(k1 \[u2264] K \[u2264] k2) for n trials of probability p (k2 defaults to k1) (also \fBB\fR)
.TP
\fBCHISQ.DIST(x, deg_freedom, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the chi-square distribution: density, or left tail (also \fBCHISQDIST\fR)
.TP
\fBCHISQ.DIST.RT(x, deg_freedom)\fR \(-> number
the chi-square right tail (also \fBCHIDIST\fR)
.TP
\fBCHISQ.INV(probability, deg_freedom)\fR \(-> number
the chi-square left-tail quantile (also \fBCHISQINV\fR)
.TP
\fBCHISQ.INV.RT(probability, deg_freedom)\fR \(-> number
the chi-square right-tail quantile (also \fBCHIINV\fR)
.TP
\fBEXPON.DIST(x, lambda, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the exponential distribution of rate \[u03BB]: density, or cumulative (also \fBEXPONDIST\fR)
.TP
\fBF.DIST(x, deg_freedom1, deg_freedom2, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the F distribution: density, or left tail
.TP
\fBF.DIST.RT(x, deg_freedom1, deg_freedom2)\fR \(-> number
the F right tail (also \fBFDIST\fR)
.TP
\fBF.INV(probability, deg_freedom1, deg_freedom2)\fR \(-> number
the F left-tail quantile
.TP
\fBF.INV.RT(probability, deg_freedom1, deg_freedom2)\fR \(-> number
the F right-tail quantile (also \fBFINV\fR)
.TP
\fBGAMMA.DIST(x, alpha, beta, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the gamma distribution of shape \[u03B1] and scale \[u03B2]: density, or cumulative (also \fBGAMMADIST\fR)
.TP
\fBGAMMA.INV(probability, alpha, beta)\fR \(-> number
the gamma quantile (also \fBGAMMAINV\fR)
.TP
\fBGAUSS(z)\fR \(-> number
\[u03A6](z) \[u2212] 0.5: the standard normal probability between 0 and z
.TP
\fBHYPGEOM.DIST(sample_s, number_sample, population_s, number_pop, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the hypergeometric probability of k successes in a sample of n from N holding K: P(X = k), or P(X \[u2264] k)
.TP
\fBHYPGEOMDIST(sample_s, number_sample, population_s, number_pop)\fR \(-> number
the hypergeometric probability P(X = k)
.TP
\fBLOGNORM.DIST(x, mean, standard_dev, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the log-normal distribution: density, or cumulative
.TP
\fBLOGNORMDIST(x, mean, standard_dev)\fR \(-> number
the cumulative log-normal distribution
.TP
\fBLOGNORM.INV(probability, mean, standard_dev)\fR \(-> number
the log-normal quantile (also \fBLOGINV\fR)
.TP
\fBNEGBINOM.DIST(number_f, number_s, probability_s, cumulative)\fR \(-> number
the negative binomial probability of f failures before the s-th success: P(F = f), or P(F \[u2264] f)
.TP
\fBNEGBINOMDIST(number_f, number_s, probability_s)\fR \(-> number
the negative binomial probability P(F = f)
.TP
