\fBR.DRAYLEIGH(x, scale, [give_log])\fR \(-> number
the density at x of the Rayleigh distribution of the given scale, or its logarithm (give_log)
.TP
\fBR.PRAYLEIGH(x, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(X \[u2264] x) for the Rayleigh distribution of the given scale, or P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QRAYLEIGH(p, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the Rayleigh distribution of the given scale: the x with P(X \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.DSNORM(x, shape, location, scale, [give_log])\fR \(-> number
the density at x of the skew-normal distribution of shape, location and scale, or its logarithm (give_log)
.TP
\fBR.PSNORM(x, shape, location, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(X \[u2264] x) for the skew-normal distribution of shape, location and scale, or P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QSNORM(p, shape, location, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the skew-normal distribution of shape, location and scale: the x with P(X \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.DST(x, df, shape, [give_log])\fR \(-> number
the density at x of the skew-t distribution of df degrees of freedom and the given shape, or its logarithm (give_log)
.TP
\fBR.PST(x, df, shape, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(X \[u2264] x) for the skew-t distribution of df degrees of freedom and the given shape, or P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QST(p, df, shape, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the skew-t distribution of df degrees of freedom and the given shape: the x with P(X \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.DT(x, df, [give_log])\fR \(-> number
the density at x of the Student t distribution of df degrees of freedom, or its logarithm (give_log)
.TP
\fBR.PT(x, df, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(X \[u2264] x) for the Student t distribution of df degrees of freedom, or P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QT(p, df, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the Student t distribution of df degrees of freedom: the x with P(X \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.DWEIBULL(x, shape, scale, [give_log])\fR \(-> number
the density at x of the Weibull distribution of shape and scale, or its logarithm (give_log)
.TP
\fBR.PWEIBULL(x, shape, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(X \[u2264] x) for the Weibull distribution of shape and scale, or P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QWEIBULL(p, shape, scale, [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the Weibull distribution of shape and scale: the x with P(X \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(X > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.PTUKEY(x, nmeans, df, [nranges=1], [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
P(Q \[u2264] x) for the largest of nranges (default 1) studentized ranges of nmeans means with df degrees of freedom, or P(Q > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), or its logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBR.QTUKEY(p, nmeans, df, [nranges=1], [lower_tail], [log_p])\fR \(-> number
the quantile of the studentized range of nmeans means with df degrees of freedom, the largest of nranges (default 1): the x with P(Q \[u2264] x) = p, or of P(Q > x) (lower_tail = FALSE), p may be a logarithm (log_p)
.TP
\fBSSMEDIAN(array OVER T, [interval=1])\fR \(-> number
the median of grouped data (Gnumeric): each value the midpoint of an interval of width interval (default 1), interpolated within the median's interval
.TP
\fBINTERPOLATION(xs, ys OVER T, target, [method], [next])\fR \(-> number
INTERPOLATION(xs, ys OVER T, target, [method], [next]): ys interpolated at target (Gnumeric): method 0 linear, 2 staircase, 4 natural cubic spline; 1, 3, 5 the same averaged over [target, next]
.TP
\fBHPFILTER(values OVER T, [lambda=1600])\fR \(-> number
each item's trend under the Hodrick\[u2013]Prescott filter with smoothing \[u03BB] (default 1600); the cycle is y \[u2212] trend; needs 6 values
.TP
\fBADTEST(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the p-value of the Anderson\[u2013]Darling test of normality (Gnumeric; 8 values or more)
.TP
\fBCVMTEST(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the p-value of the Cram\[u00E9]r\[u2013]von Mises test of normality (Gnumeric; 8 values or more)
.TP
\fBLKSTEST(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the p-value of the Lilliefors (Kolmogorov\[u2013]Smirnov) test of normality (Gnumeric; 5 values or more)
.TP
\fBSFTEST(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the p-value of the Shapiro\[u2013]Francia test of normality (Gnumeric; 5 to 5000 values)
.TP
\fBADTEST.STAT(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the Anderson\[u2013]Darling statistic A\[u00B2] (adjusted for n), Gnumeric's ADTEST second value
.TP
\fBCVMTEST.STAT(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the Cram\[u00E9]r\[u2013]von Mises statistic W\[u00B2], Gnumeric's CVMTEST second value
.TP
\fBLKSTEST.STAT(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the Lilliefors statistic D, Gnumeric's LKSTEST second value
.TP
\fBSFTEST.STAT(values OVER T)\fR \(-> number
the Shapiro\[u2013]Francia statistic W', Gnumeric's SFTEST second value
.TP
\fBLOGREG(known_ys, known_xs OVER T, [affine], [stats] INTO K)\fR \(-> number
LOGREG(known_ys, known_xs OVER T, [affine], [stats] INTO K): the least-squares fit y = m\[u00B7]ln x + b (Gnumeric): m, b (affine = FALSE: b = 0); with stats LINEST's 5\[u00D7]2 block for ln x, row by row
.TP
\fBLOGFIT(known_ys, known_xs OVER T INTO K)\fR \(-> number
LOGFIT(known_ys, known_xs OVER T INTO K): the fit y = a + b\[u00B7]ln(sign\[u00B7](x \[u2212] c)) by Gnumeric's trial-and-error search: sign, a, b, c and the sum of squared residuals; #NUM! if the points are not logarithmic enough
.TP
\fBPERIODOGRAM(y OVER T, [filter] INTO K)\fR \(-> number
PERIODOGRAM(y OVER T, [filter] INTO K): the magnitudes of the first half of the Fourier transform (Gnumeric), after a window: 0 none, 1 Bartlett, 2 Hann, 3 Welch
.TP
\fBCRONBACH(x1, x2, \[u2026] OVER T)\fR \(-> number
CRONBACH(x1, x2, \[u2026] OVER T): Cronbach's \[u03B1] of the items x1, x2, \[u2026] (one row per respondent; population variances, as Gnumeric)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation]): the additive Holt\[u2013]Winters (triple exponential smoothing) forecast at target_date, LibreOffice's algorithm; seasonality 1 detects the season, 0 none; a target_date before the timeline's end is #NUM! (also \fBFORECAST.ETS.ADD\fR)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.MULT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.MULT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, \[u2026]): FORECAST.ETS with multiplicative seasons (LibreOffice)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.CONFINT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [confidence_level=0.95], [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.CONFINT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [confidence_level], [seasonality], \[u2026]): the half-width of the prediction interval (default confidence_level 0.95); only for seasonality 0 (LibreOffice simulates seasonal intervals randomly): otherwise #N/A (also \fBFORECAST.ETS.PI.ADD\fR)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.PI.MULT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, [confidence_level=0.95], [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.PI.MULT(target_date, values, timeline OVER T, \[u2026]): FORECAST.ETS.CONFINT for the multiplicative model (the same for seasonality 0, the only one supported)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.STAT(values, timeline OVER T, statistic_type, [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.STAT(values, timeline OVER T, statistic_type, \[u2026]): a statistic of FORECAST.ETS's fit: 1 \[u03B1], 2 \[u03B2] (trend), 3 \[u03B3] (season), 4 MASE, 5 SMAPE, 6 MAE, 7 RMSE, 8 step, 9 seasonality (also \fBFORECAST.ETS.STAT.ADD\fR)
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.STAT.MULT(values, timeline OVER T, statistic_type, [seasonality], [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.STAT.MULT(values, timeline OVER T, statistic_type, \[u2026]): FORECAST.ETS.STAT of the multiplicative model
.TP
\fBFORECAST.ETS.SEASONALITY(values, timeline OVER T, [data_completion], [aggregation])\fR \(-> number
FORECAST.ETS.SEASONALITY(values, timeline OVER T, [data_completion], [aggregation]): the season length FORECAST.ETS detects (1: none)
.TP
\fBLEVERAGE(A OVER R, C INTO K)\fR \(-> number
LEVERAGE(A OVER R, C INTO K): the regression leverages, the diagonal of A\[u00B7](A\[u1D40]A)\[u207B]\[u00B9]\[u00B7]A\[u1D40], one per row of A (Gnumeric); #NUM! if A's columns are dependent
